  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(x)$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(-x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}(-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(x)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(x)$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi }{3}}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$",
